TITLE:: PltSpectrogram
summary:: Scrolling spectrogram: time across, frequency up, magnitude as brightness
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltSpectrum, Classes/PltWave, Classes/FreqScope

DESCRIPTION::
link::Classes/FreqScope:: is one spectral slice moving in time. This keeps the
history, which is where the structure of a sound actually shows.

Each frame writes strong::one column:: into an link::Classes/Image:: and the image
is blitted, rather than a UserView redrawing several hundred rects per frame. The
columns ring buffer, and drawing takes two pieces so the newest column is always at
the right edge.

note::
Brightness is mapped through strong::gamma::, not linearly. A noise floor 30 dB up
from code::floorDb:: reads as half ink under a linear map and the whole picture
washes out.
::

note::
On a logarithmic axis the low end has many rows per bin: at 2048 points there are
nine bins below 200 Hz. Repeating the bin draws them as horizontal bands, so a row
narrower than a bin interpolates between neighbours instead.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Live analysis of a bus.
argument:: bus
argument:: fftSize
argument:: server
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: fromSoundFile
A whole file, analysed offline in the language with link::Classes/Signal#-fft::, so
no server is needed and the result is the same on every run. Multichannel files are
mixed to mono, since a spectrogram of one channel of a stereo file misleads.
argument:: path
argument:: fftSize
argument:: hop
Fraction of a window between frames.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: framesFromSoundFile
The analysis on its own: code::(frames:, duration:, sampleRate:)::, where frames is
an Array of magnitude arrays.

METHOD:: column
One column of the picture as values from 0 to 1, top row first. Pure, so the row
mapping can be tested without a window.
argument:: frame
argument:: rows
argument:: minHz
argument:: maxHz
argument:: floorDb
argument:: ceilDb
argument:: logFreq
argument:: sampleRate
argument:: fftSize

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: start
METHOD:: stop
The analysis synth and the poller. Rebuilt through link::Classes/ServerTree::, so
they come back after cmd-period.

METHOD:: pushFrame
One frame of magnitudes, written as one column. Anything can call it.

METHOD:: logFreq
Logarithmic frequency axis, labelled where a listener thinks. Changing it clears
the history, since the row mapping is no longer the same.

METHOD:: span
Seconds visible.
METHOD:: minHz
Bottom of a logarithmic axis.
METHOD:: maxHz
METHOD:: floorDb
METHOD:: ceilDb
METHOD:: gamma
Brightness curve. Higher darkens the floor and leaves the peaks.
METHOD:: pollRate
Frames per second, which also sets how many columns a span holds.
METHOD:: mags
The most recent frame.
METHOD:: sampleRate
The file's rate offline, the server's when live. Getting this wrong puts every
partial on the wrong row.
METHOD:: bus
METHOD:: fftSize
METHOD:: server

EXAMPLES::
code::
// a whole file, offline
PltSpectrogram.fromSoundFile(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav").front;

// the same on a log axis, where formants read as shapes
(
PltSpectrogram.fromSoundFile(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav", 2048)
	.logFreq_(true).front;
)

// live
s.boot;
x = PltSpectrogram(0).front;
y = { Blip.ar(LFNoise0.kr(2).exprange(80, 400), 20, 0.2) ! 2 }.play;
x.span_(16);           // a longer window
x.gamma_(3);           // a darker floor
y.free; x.close;
::
